1
00:00:00,831 --> 00:00:02,399
Four months ago,

2
00:00:02,399 --> 00:00:06,803
my house was raided by the agents of the Venezuelan regime.

3
00:00:06,803 --> 00:00:09,673
They destroyed the walls,

4
00:00:09,673 --> 00:00:11,909
the ceilings

5
00:00:11,909 --> 00:00:15,779
and even killed my dogs.

6
00:00:15,779 --> 00:00:19,216
After that, they decided to take away my Venezuelan nationality,

7
00:00:19,216 --> 00:00:23,954
something that is prohibited by the Venezuelan Constitution.

8
00:00:23,954 --> 00:00:28,125
What sparked this was a public statement I made

9
00:00:28,125 --> 00:00:30,527
supporting the law enforcement

10
00:00:30,527 --> 00:00:33,897
and military buildup against Nicolás Maduro

11
00:00:33,897 --> 00:00:35,832
that led to his capture

12
00:00:35,832 --> 00:00:37,801
on January 3.

13
00:00:37,801 --> 00:00:39,269
And maybe the fact

14
00:00:39,269 --> 00:00:45,509
that my house was only blocks away from Delcy Rodríguez’s,

15
00:00:45,509 --> 00:00:48,745
interim dictator of Venezuela.

16
00:00:48,745 --> 00:00:50,614
For my family and I,

17
00:00:50,614 --> 00:00:54,017
the journey of persecution has been very long.

18
00:00:54,017 --> 00:00:58,221
I was elected mayor of Caracas for eight years.

19
00:00:58,221 --> 00:01:01,758
After that, I was banned to run for office.

20
00:01:01,758 --> 00:01:04,528
I had three assassination attempts,

21
00:01:04,528 --> 00:01:07,397
and in the year 2014,

22
00:01:07,397 --> 00:01:10,167
when Maduro stole his first election,

23
00:01:10,167 --> 00:01:12,836
we called for protests, peaceful protests.

24
00:01:12,836 --> 00:01:15,906
People came out by the thousands,

25
00:01:15,906 --> 00:01:18,642
and Maduro called for my arrest.

26
00:01:18,642 --> 00:01:22,379
I went into hiding, and I had three choices.

27
00:01:22,379 --> 00:01:24,648
I could stay in hiding.

28
00:01:24,648 --> 00:01:26,450
I could leave the country.

29
00:01:26,450 --> 00:01:28,685
Or I could turn myself in.

30
00:01:28,685 --> 00:01:31,989
So I decided to turn myself in.

31
00:01:31,989 --> 00:01:39,196
And I became a political prisoner for seven years.

32
00:01:39,196 --> 00:01:41,398
In solitary confinement,

33
00:01:41,398 --> 00:01:45,869
the sound I remember most is not screaming.

34
00:01:45,869 --> 00:01:47,204
It's the locks.

35
00:01:47,204 --> 00:01:50,874
One lock, then another, then another.

36
00:01:50,874 --> 00:01:53,810
Nine locks in total.

37
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:56,980
I was in complete confinement,

38
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:59,983
so locks became very present to me,

39
00:01:59,983 --> 00:02:04,888
and they became the framework through which I started to think about freedom,

40
00:02:04,888 --> 00:02:08,625
which was the reason I was in prison.

41
00:02:08,625 --> 00:02:10,894
I started to think about locks

42
00:02:10,894 --> 00:02:16,566
and the framework as the things were taken away from me.

43
00:02:16,566 --> 00:02:19,002
My speech was taken away from me,

44
00:02:19,002 --> 00:02:21,805
a lock on my freedom of speech.

45
00:02:21,805 --> 00:02:26,877
My freedom to mobility, a lock on my mobility.

46
00:02:26,877 --> 00:02:31,048
The freedom to have a free and fair trial,

47
00:02:31,048 --> 00:02:34,785
a lock on my freedom and justice.

48
00:02:34,785 --> 00:02:36,086
When they took my home,

49
00:02:36,086 --> 00:02:37,454
a lock on my property.

50
00:02:37,454 --> 00:02:39,022
When they took my nationality,

51
00:02:39,022 --> 00:02:41,358
a lock on my identity.

52
00:02:41,358 --> 00:02:45,028
So I started to think about locks as the problem

53
00:02:45,028 --> 00:02:48,398
and keys as the solution.

54
00:02:48,398 --> 00:02:51,802
While in prison, in solitary confinement,

55
00:02:51,802 --> 00:02:56,339
I figured out a way to speak to other prisoners.

56
00:02:56,339 --> 00:02:59,776
The only moment I could see other prisoners

57
00:02:59,776 --> 00:03:02,846
was when I was able to go to mass.

58
00:03:02,846 --> 00:03:07,718
So when I was in mass with two details sitting next to me,

59
00:03:07,718 --> 00:03:09,886
there was only one moment

60
00:03:09,886 --> 00:03:13,557
when we stood up and gave the hug of peace.

61
00:03:13,557 --> 00:03:14,958
And when that happened,

62
00:03:14,958 --> 00:03:17,360
I would stand up

63
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:21,031
and tell those prisoners that I knew that were with me,

64
00:03:21,031 --> 00:03:22,999
I would tell them,

65
00:03:22,999 --> 00:03:25,168
"Peace be with you, my brother.

66
00:03:25,168 --> 00:03:29,172
Please tell your wife to find my wife."

67
00:03:29,172 --> 00:03:32,909
And in that way, we developed a protocol to communicate within prison.

68
00:03:32,909 --> 00:03:35,846
It took time, a lot of iterations.

69
00:03:35,846 --> 00:03:37,414
But over six months,

70
00:03:37,414 --> 00:03:40,050
we were able to figure out a way

71
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:42,419
to take control of the prison.

72
00:03:42,419 --> 00:03:45,922
A year after we started, there was a spark.

73
00:03:45,922 --> 00:03:50,460
And that spark led to the activation of the protocol.

74
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:51,862
And for 12 hours,

75
00:03:51,862 --> 00:03:55,732
we took complete control of that prison.

76
00:03:55,732 --> 00:04:01,204
After that, things became even more lonely and more difficult.

77
00:04:01,204 --> 00:04:04,808
I went [on] a hunger strike for 28 days.

78
00:04:04,808 --> 00:04:07,978
I lost 14 kilograms,

79
00:04:07,978 --> 00:04:11,782
and then they took everything away from me.

80
00:04:11,782 --> 00:04:15,685
Even my books, which were my universe.

81
00:04:15,685 --> 00:04:18,421
So I was in complete loneliness.

82
00:04:18,421 --> 00:04:20,957
And in that complete loneliness,

83
00:04:20,957 --> 00:04:26,096
I understood that the one thing they were not able to take away from me

84
00:04:26,096 --> 00:04:29,466
was my heart and my soul.

85
00:04:29,466 --> 00:04:34,137
The way I felt and the way I thought.

86
00:04:34,137 --> 00:04:38,909
It was a key to my own internal freedom.

87
00:04:38,909 --> 00:04:41,077
By the end of 2020,

88
00:04:41,077 --> 00:04:42,879
I was able to escape Venezuela --

89
00:04:42,879 --> 00:04:45,315
something I never wanted to do.

90
00:04:45,315 --> 00:04:48,618
We had to cross several checkpoints,

91
00:04:48,618 --> 00:04:52,989
and finally I crossed a river and went into Colombia.

92
00:04:52,989 --> 00:04:54,891
And I began exile.

93
00:04:54,891 --> 00:04:58,361
Exile is another form of imprisonment.

94
00:04:58,361 --> 00:05:02,265
And I came out into a very different world.

95
00:05:02,265 --> 00:05:05,268
I came out into a world of COVID,

96
00:05:05,268 --> 00:05:09,339
and I also came out into a very different world in terms of technology.

97
00:05:09,339 --> 00:05:11,875
I had gone into prison with a BlackBerry.

98
00:05:11,875 --> 00:05:13,777
(Laughter)

99
00:05:13,777 --> 00:05:16,847
And I came out into the world of smartphones,

100
00:05:16,847 --> 00:05:19,950
voice notes, video calls

101
00:05:19,950 --> 00:05:21,218
and cryptocurrency.

102
00:05:21,218 --> 00:05:24,020
None of that was part of my existence before.

103
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:26,189
It was shocking.

104
00:05:26,189 --> 00:05:27,657
Some months after that,

105
00:05:27,657 --> 00:05:31,127
I was a visiting fellow at Stanford,

106
00:05:31,127 --> 00:05:33,997
and I made a very good friend and he told me,

107
00:05:33,997 --> 00:05:35,298
"Listen, man,

108
00:05:35,298 --> 00:05:39,436
the most important thing that happened while you were away

109
00:05:39,436 --> 00:05:42,339
was Bitcoin and AI.

110
00:05:42,339 --> 00:05:45,408
Bitcoin, because it's open-source,

111
00:05:45,408 --> 00:05:47,010
decentralized money

112
00:05:47,010 --> 00:05:50,247
that you can hold by having your own keys.

113
00:05:50,247 --> 00:05:51,481
And AI,

114
00:05:51,481 --> 00:05:54,017
because it's going to revolutionize everything,

115
00:05:54,017 --> 00:05:59,489
the way we use [and] process information.

116
00:05:59,489 --> 00:06:02,492
So I became very interested,

117
00:06:02,492 --> 00:06:04,928
not about the technology itself,

118
00:06:04,928 --> 00:06:07,464
because I've never written a line of code in my life.

119
00:06:07,464 --> 00:06:10,867
I didn't know what open-source or decentralized technology was,

120
00:06:10,867 --> 00:06:13,503
but I became interested in the intersection

121
00:06:13,503 --> 00:06:18,642
between technology and freedom.

122
00:06:18,642 --> 00:06:20,477
And some months after that,

123
00:06:20,477 --> 00:06:23,613
I started to meet other people.

124
00:06:23,613 --> 00:06:28,385
Other people like myself that had been political prisoners,

125
00:06:28,385 --> 00:06:31,788
that had led protests in their own countries,

126
00:06:31,788 --> 00:06:34,257
that had been forced into exile.

127
00:06:34,257 --> 00:06:38,662
We were very different -- skin color, religion, languages --

128
00:06:38,662 --> 00:06:44,000
but we were very similar in our struggle for freedom.

129
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,072
So we decided to create the World Liberty Congress,

130
00:06:49,072 --> 00:06:53,710
which has become the largest global alliance of freedom fighters

131
00:06:53,710 --> 00:06:55,979
and democracy defenders.

132
00:06:55,979 --> 00:06:58,281
60 countries are represented --

133
00:06:58,281 --> 00:07:02,919
(Applause)

134
00:07:02,919 --> 00:07:07,590
60 countries represented in the World Liberty Congress.

135
00:07:07,590 --> 00:07:11,661
And that gave me the opportunity to get to know a lot of people.

136
00:07:11,661 --> 00:07:15,665
It gave me the opportunity not just to go to different countries,

137
00:07:15,665 --> 00:07:19,302
but to share, with different people, about their experiences.

138
00:07:19,302 --> 00:07:21,905
And particularly about the way

139
00:07:21,905 --> 00:07:27,911
in which technology was being used to fight for freedom.

140
00:07:27,911 --> 00:07:30,981
So rather than tell you about the technology,

141
00:07:30,981 --> 00:07:33,083
I want to tell you about some of the people

142
00:07:33,083 --> 00:07:36,119
that have been using this technology.

143
00:07:36,119 --> 00:07:39,122
Maria is a Venezuelan nurse.

144
00:07:39,122 --> 00:07:42,792
During COVID, she was earning $3 per month.

145
00:07:42,792 --> 00:07:47,197
So we figured out a program for nurses and doctors

146
00:07:47,197 --> 00:07:51,301
to get a $100 monthly support.

147
00:07:51,301 --> 00:07:52,502
But we had a problem.

148
00:07:52,502 --> 00:07:57,307
The financial system was taken by the regime.

149
00:07:57,307 --> 00:07:59,509
It was surveilled by the regime.

150
00:07:59,509 --> 00:08:02,545
So we turned to stablecoins and Bitcoin,

151
00:08:02,545 --> 00:08:04,381
and we were able to support

152
00:08:04,381 --> 00:08:09,052
more than 70,000 medical doctors and nurses

153
00:08:09,052 --> 00:08:11,988
without the intermediation of the regime.

154
00:08:11,988 --> 00:08:15,792
(Applause)

155
00:08:15,792 --> 00:08:20,163
It was not just technology, it was a life support.

156
00:08:20,163 --> 00:08:26,903
It was a way for them to survive in the moment of largest crisis.

157
00:08:26,903 --> 00:08:30,206
Then another friend, Anyan.

158
00:08:30,206 --> 00:08:32,075
Anyan is a journalist.

159
00:08:32,075 --> 00:08:36,579
He's dedicated his career to following conflict zones,

160
00:08:36,579 --> 00:08:40,417
from Congo to Rwanda to Mexico.

161
00:08:40,417 --> 00:08:45,255
And today he has a network of over a thousand journalists.

162
00:08:45,255 --> 00:08:46,890
But they have a problem.

163
00:08:46,890 --> 00:08:51,094
How do they keep their own information without someone switching it off

164
00:08:51,094 --> 00:08:53,329
or deleting their information?

165
00:08:53,329 --> 00:08:55,665
So they turn to Nostr.

166
00:08:55,665 --> 00:08:58,935
Nostr is to communication what Bitcoin is to money.

167
00:08:58,935 --> 00:09:03,440
It's an open-source protocol that nobody owns,

168
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:05,909
you can have your own information,

169
00:09:05,909 --> 00:09:07,977
you can have your own followers,

170
00:09:07,977 --> 00:09:10,046
you can publish your information,

171
00:09:10,046 --> 00:09:13,850
and no one can delete that information.

172
00:09:13,850 --> 00:09:15,251
Last January,

173
00:09:15,251 --> 00:09:19,322
there was an election in Uganda.

174
00:09:19,322 --> 00:09:21,224
My very good friend Bobi Wine,

175
00:09:21,224 --> 00:09:23,726
founding member of the World Liberty Congress,

176
00:09:23,726 --> 00:09:25,228
was going to win the election.

177
00:09:25,228 --> 00:09:29,466
There was no doubt that he was going to win the election.

178
00:09:29,466 --> 00:09:31,334
But there was also no doubt

179
00:09:31,334 --> 00:09:34,404
that they were going to steal the election.

180
00:09:34,404 --> 00:09:36,106
And in order to steal the election,

181
00:09:36,106 --> 00:09:38,575
one of the things that the regime was going to do

182
00:09:38,575 --> 00:09:42,145
was to shut down the internet.

183
00:09:42,145 --> 00:09:45,215
So I told Bob, I said,

184
00:09:45,215 --> 00:09:47,951
"My friend, there is something called BitChat."

185
00:09:47,951 --> 00:09:53,056
BitChat is an open-source protocol that connect phones

186
00:09:53,056 --> 00:09:56,159
without internet, without cell towers,

187
00:09:56,159 --> 00:10:00,663
just by Bluetooth mesh network, you are able to connect people.

188
00:10:00,663 --> 00:10:04,667
So for 21 days, Bobi posted about BitChat.

189
00:10:04,667 --> 00:10:12,008
And by Election Day, 1.8 million people in Uganda had downloaded this protocol.

190
00:10:12,008 --> 00:10:13,209
And this protocol --

191
00:10:13,209 --> 00:10:15,178
(Applause)

192
00:10:15,178 --> 00:10:17,113
And this protocol became their internet.

193
00:10:17,113 --> 00:10:20,083
It became the way for them to communicate.

194
00:10:20,083 --> 00:10:22,986
And it was very powerful.

195
00:10:22,986 --> 00:10:26,089
But Uganda also showed me something else.

196
00:10:26,089 --> 00:10:28,191
We had a backup plan.

197
00:10:28,191 --> 00:10:32,328
So we had helped to smuggle more than 30 Starlinks into Uganda

198
00:10:32,328 --> 00:10:36,032
before the election.

199
00:10:36,032 --> 00:10:40,637
But the regime knew that something could happen.

200
00:10:40,637 --> 00:10:45,074
So they wrote to Starlink asking for the service to be blocked.

201
00:10:45,074 --> 00:10:46,910
And it was blocked.

202
00:10:46,910 --> 00:10:50,880
The service was blocked, and it was not able to be used.

203
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:53,883
But that same week, that same week,

204
00:10:53,883 --> 00:10:56,953
in Iran, there were protesters.

205
00:10:56,953 --> 00:11:02,559
And Starlink was open, the same way it was in Ukraine.

206
00:11:02,559 --> 00:11:04,127
So what that showed me

207
00:11:04,127 --> 00:11:06,162
is that the technology is not the problem.

208
00:11:06,162 --> 00:11:07,564
The technology is there.

209
00:11:07,564 --> 00:11:10,133
Starlink can use, or any other technology.

210
00:11:10,133 --> 00:11:11,968
The problem was very clear.

211
00:11:11,968 --> 00:11:14,837
The problem was the gatekeeper.

212
00:11:14,837 --> 00:11:18,675
Whenever there is a gatekeeper, there is a problem.

213
00:11:18,675 --> 00:11:23,012
It doesn't matter if the gatekeeper is a government, if it's a bank,

214
00:11:23,012 --> 00:11:24,314
if it's a company.

215
00:11:24,314 --> 00:11:29,118
The problem is the gate itself.

216
00:11:29,118 --> 00:11:35,124
So what we need to figure out is how we can have these keys

217
00:11:35,124 --> 00:11:39,128
and the elephant in the room in any conversation today about AI is:

218
00:11:39,128 --> 00:11:41,598
who holds the keys?

219
00:11:41,598 --> 00:11:44,234
Who holds the keys to your own information?

220
00:11:44,234 --> 00:11:46,936
[Do] you hold the information or [does] a corporation

221
00:11:46,936 --> 00:11:48,905
hold that information?

222
00:11:48,905 --> 00:11:52,976
Fortunately, in the same way that Bitcoin is working for money

223
00:11:52,976 --> 00:11:56,879
or Nostr for communication, or BitChat for connectivity,

224
00:11:56,879 --> 00:11:58,848
today there is sovereign AI.

225
00:11:58,848 --> 00:12:01,851
AI that you can hold in your own machine.

226
00:12:01,851 --> 00:12:06,689
AI that you can manage with your own privacy and that you can decide.

227
00:12:06,689 --> 00:12:09,192
Today hold my own sovereign agent,

228
00:12:09,192 --> 00:12:14,764
and I am working with other people for them in their autocratic countries

229
00:12:14,764 --> 00:12:16,666
to have their own agent

230
00:12:16,666 --> 00:12:19,302
that is completely sovereign.

231
00:12:19,302 --> 00:12:23,273
But keys are hard to master.

232
00:12:23,273 --> 00:12:25,842
I remember when I was in prison,

233
00:12:25,842 --> 00:12:31,648
waking up to an assault rifle pointed [at] my head.

234
00:12:31,648 --> 00:12:36,686
And several men in the cell just tearing everything apart.

235
00:12:36,686 --> 00:12:39,088
I was very scared.

236
00:12:39,088 --> 00:12:46,829
And the way that fear felt was my heart was pounding.

237
00:12:46,829 --> 00:12:51,434
And they came back again every week, every other week.

238
00:12:51,434 --> 00:12:55,405
So I decided that I needed to do something about it.

239
00:12:55,405 --> 00:13:01,110
And what I decided to do was to figure out how I could control my heart.

240
00:13:01,110 --> 00:13:04,380
Not spiritually, not intellectually,

241
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:07,150
but physically control my heart.

242
00:13:07,150 --> 00:13:10,653
So I decided to put together this drill.

243
00:13:10,653 --> 00:13:14,190
I would do push-ups, burpees, jumping jacks.

244
00:13:14,190 --> 00:13:17,527
I would take my heart rate to 80, 90 percent,

245
00:13:17,527 --> 00:13:19,562
and then I would sit down.

246
00:13:19,562 --> 00:13:21,130
Sit still,

247
00:13:21,130 --> 00:13:22,298
breathe

248
00:13:22,298 --> 00:13:26,569
and try to take my heart rate as low as I could,

249
00:13:26,569 --> 00:13:29,539
as fast as I could.

250
00:13:29,539 --> 00:13:33,409
The agents came back to my cell many times.

251
00:13:33,409 --> 00:13:35,478
But every time they came,

252
00:13:35,478 --> 00:13:37,013
I felt control.

253
00:13:37,013 --> 00:13:41,451
Because I had found my own internal key to self-control

254
00:13:41,451 --> 00:13:43,519
and to freedom.

255
00:13:43,519 --> 00:13:47,490
And [in] that same way I was able to find that self-control

256
00:13:47,490 --> 00:13:49,258
and my own internal key,

257
00:13:49,258 --> 00:13:54,564
you have the choice to have your keys to your sovereignty

258
00:13:54,564 --> 00:13:58,201
in the 21st century and in the digital age.

259
00:13:58,201 --> 00:14:02,705
(Applause)

260
00:14:02,705 --> 00:14:06,442
But keys need a purpose.

261
00:14:06,442 --> 00:14:08,845
My purpose, for years,

262
00:14:08,845 --> 00:14:13,416
has been the freedom of my country, Venezuela.

263
00:14:13,416 --> 00:14:18,988
I hold my keys very closely, but I hold them with the purpose.

264
00:14:18,988 --> 00:14:24,127
And for me, the purpose is the freedom of my country, Venezuela,

265
00:14:24,127 --> 00:14:26,429
and the freedom of every country

266
00:14:26,429 --> 00:14:29,899
